KNOWNBUG libcxx
sort.cpp
--cpp17 --stdlib libc++ --unwind 8 --object-bits 12
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ sorted: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
no body for callee
--
libc++ (LLVM 18) <algorithm>: std::sort on int has no body for
std::__sort<__less<int, int> &, int *>.  libc++ declares that specialization
`extern template' (sort.h:905, [temp.explicit]/12: an explicit instantiation
declaration suppresses implicit instantiation; the definition is in
libc++.so).  The definition is visible in the header, so the front end
could instantiate it for verification, as it does for user code (a
header-free `extern template' works).  Existing tests such as
cpp20_algorithm_basic_libcxx accept either verdict and hide this.
clang++ 18 -stdlib=libc++ accepts this program, and it fails only its
WRONG assertion.
